## What this predicate means

**A `has-range` B** — A's target-type (range) is B.  Applied to predicate memorials: the predicate's range is the class of entity that can SERVE AS the object of the relation.

Examples:
- `:mm.memory/cites has-range :mm/Memory` (citation target must be a memorial)
- `:mm.memory/created-by has-range :mm/Actor` (creator must be an actor)
- `:mm.memory/has-domain has-range :dt/Class` (the predicate's domain is itself a class)

## Substrate identity

- **Slot ident**: `{{predicate-ident}}`
- **Domain**: `{{domain}}` (typically `:dt/Property`)
- **Range**: `{{range}}` (typically `:dt/Class`)
- **Cardinality**: `{{cardinality}}` (typically `:many` — a predicate may have multiple range classes via disjunction)
- **Sub-property of**: `:dt/property-range-relation`

## Formal signature

`{{predicate-name}} : {{domain}} × {{range}} → Bool`

## When to use

Declare `has-range:` when:
- A is a predicate / property definition
- B is the class restricting which entities can serve as objects of A
- Audit invariants on conformance can verify (target-of-edge ∈ range)

## Audit invariants

- "predicate: domain/range conformance" — for every fact `x A y`, `y` must be an instance of the declared range of A (or its subclass).  RDFS rule rdfs3.

Cross-check via `/memory-audit`.

## Formal ontology mappings

Aligned predicates from established formal vocabularies:

- **RDFS** `rdfs:range` — exact semantic match (the canonical range-restriction predicate; entailment via rdfs3)
- **OWL** `rdfs:range` — same (OWL uses RDFS's range predicate directly)
- **SHACL** `sh:class` (within a property shape) — close analogue (shape-validation context)
- **DC Terms** — no direct equivalent
- **PROV-O** — no direct equivalent
- **SKOS** — no direct equivalent

